WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.536 --> 00:00:02.240
(gentle music)

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.280
- [Narrator] Like buried treasure,

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.373
they lie hidden from sight.

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:11.600
Echoes of an ancient
past, they whisper secrets

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.463
and tell tales once lost to time.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.440
Fossils are remnants or
impressions of ancient organisms

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.950
that are naturally preserved in stone.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.270
While there are hundreds of fossil types,

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.570
they are often grouped
into two major categories,

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:34.080
body fossils, which are
the preserved remains

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.240
of plants and animals, and trace fossils,

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.560
which are records of an animal's behavior,

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.470
such as footprints.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.119
Together, they form the fossil record,

00:00:44.119 --> 00:00:46.562
a primary account that tells the story

00:00:46.562 --> 00:00:48.943
of life on Earth through stone.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.830
Fossilization or the process of preserving

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:58.493
organisms in stone, can
occur in countless ways.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.250
These methods are largely grouped

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.040
depending on whether the organisms

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.670
are altered during the
fossilization process.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.970
Fossilization that does
not alter a specimen

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.860
can help to preserve its
original form and texture.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.280
Among many methods, this
group includes organisms

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.930
that have been frozen,

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.713
preserved in tar pits, and mummified.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:27.420
One special case involves
trapping organisms,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.740
oftentimes insects, in amber.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.810
This process begins when
an organism is covered

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.240
in tree sap.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.060
The sap or resin forms a protective seal

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.230
around the entrapped organism.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.400
Over time, the soft resin hardens

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.800
and turns into amber

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.710
with the organism suspended within.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.890
This process creates a
biologically inert tomb

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:54.960
for the organism,
allowing its soft tissues

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.743
to be remarkably preserved.

00:01:59.826 --> 00:02:03.210
Other fossilization
methods change the specimen

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.230
as it is being preserved.

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:09.270
For instance, carbonization
transforms soft tissues

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.910
into thin black films of carbon.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.510
In fact, countless layers
of carbonized plant material

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.823
create a well known fossil fuel, coal.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.750
But one of the most common
types of fossilization

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.593
that changes a specimen is
called permineralization.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:32.040
Permineralization begins
when minerals from water

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.950
or the ground enter
the pores of dead plant

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.210
or animal material.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:40.540
Over time, the minerals attach themselves,

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.580
clinging onto cellular walls

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:46.490
and building a crystalline
network in the empty cavities.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:49.040
This mineralization hardens the bone

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.200
and turns it into stone,

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.023
thereby preserving its original
structure in fossil form.

00:02:57.445 --> 00:02:59.057
When conditions are right,

00:02:59.057 --> 00:03:02.348
fossilization can preserve
crucial information

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:03.873
about an organism.

00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:09.560
Permineralized wood often
contains enough information

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.614
to identify its tree genus

00:03:11.614 --> 00:03:13.943
and sometimes its species.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.920
Insects encased in amber
have been so well preserved

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.940
that their genetic material was extracted

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.573
and partially sequenced.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:27.010
And footprints left
behind by ancient hominids

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:29.390
help paint a picture of what life was like

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:33.073
for early human ancestors
millions of years ago.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.260
With every fossil uncovered,

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:39.480
the planet's ancient past becomes clearer,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.842
helping shape our understanding
of our world today.

00:03:42.842 --> 00:03:45.425
(gentle music)

